    "objects": {
      "general": [
        {
          "properties": {
            "paragraphs": [
              {
                "textRuns": [
                  {
                    "value": "Opinion",
                    "textStyle": {
                      "fontFamily": "Georgia",
                      "fontSize": "15pt",
                      "color": "#1F3A5F",
                      "fontWeight": "bold"
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "textRuns": [
                  {
                    "value": " ",
                    "textStyle": {
                      "fontSize": "6pt"
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "textRuns": [
                  {
                    "value": "I side with Hamilton. The question wasn't whether the allocation was tidy; it was whether the new federal government could take a $21.5 million problem off thirteen balance sheets and make it one credible obligation. It did: 85% of the authorized sum was taken up; two of the three states that came in over quota (MA, SC) were the two Hamilton's schedule ranked as owing the most; and within three years the debt was funded and, despite the 1792 panic, U.S. bonds traded near or above par.",
                    "textStyle": {
                      "fontFamily": "Segoe UI",
                      "fontSize": "11pt",
                      "color": "#22262B"
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "textRuns": [
                  {
                    "value": " ",
                    "textStyle": {
                      "fontSize": "6pt"
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "textRuns": [
                  {
                    "value": "The method was rough, and I'd say so in the workpapers. Six of thirteen states filed returns; four got quotas by round number; MA and SC were cut by a quarter. But Hamilton was allocating with the data that existed in January 1790, and he said so.",
                    "textStyle": {
                      "fontFamily": "Segoe UI",
                      "fontSize": "11pt",
                      "color": "#22262B"
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "textRuns": [
                  {
                    "value": " ",
                    "textStyle": {
                      "fontSize": "6pt"
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "textRuns": [
                  {
                    "value": "The 1793 settlement vindicated him. The audit Madison demanded found the biggest recipients (MA, SC) were also the biggest creditors of the Union. Madison's own Virginia turned out to owe the Union $100,879 - 12 cents a head, break-even as the Compromise intended - while receiving $2.9M of relief.",
                    "textStyle": {
                      "fontFamily": "Segoe UI",
                      "fontSize": "11pt",
                      "color": "#22262B"
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "textRuns": [
                  {
                    "value": " ",
                    "textStyle": {
                      "fontSize": "6pt"
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "textRuns": [
                  {
                    "value": "Where I'd push back: the claim that federal bonds would bind creditors to the new government can't be tested at the state level, and I don't assume it. What the numbers do show is a fair outcome, reconciled in about three and a half years (Jan 1790 report to Jun 1793 settlement) - faster than Puerto Rico (~6 yrs), Greece (8) or Argentina (15).",
                    "textStyle": {
                      "fontFamily": "Segoe UI",
                      "fontSize": "11pt",
                      "color": "#22262B"
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "textRuns": [
                  {
                    "value": " ",
                    "textStyle": {
                      "fontSize": "6pt"
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "textRuns": [
                  {
                    "value": "Basis of preparation",
                    "textStyle": {
                      "fontFamily": "Georgia",
                      "fontSize": "13pt",
                      "color": "#1F3A5F",
                      "fontWeight": "bold"
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "textRuns": [
                  {
                    "value": " ",
                    "textStyle": {
                      "fontSize": "6pt"
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "textRuns": [
                  {
                    "value": "Five primary ledgers, each footed before use (Schedule B). One does not foot: two line errors in the 1792 subscription table ($500,000 and $30,000), reconciled and disclosed.",
                    "textStyle": {
                      "fontFamily": "Segoe UI",
                      "fontSize": "10pt",
                      "color": "#7A8290"
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "textRuns": [
                  {
                    "value": " ",
                    "textStyle": {
                      "fontSize": "6pt"
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "textRuns": [
                  {
                    "value": "1790 specie dollars; 2025 dollars at 36.3x CPI. $21.5M was ~11% of 1790 GDP (a modern reconstruction), about $3.4 trillion in today's economy.",
                    "textStyle": {
                      "fontFamily": "Segoe UI",
                      "fontSize": "10pt",
                      "color": "#7A8290"
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "textRuns": [
                  {
                    "value": " ",
                    "textStyle": {
                      "fontSize": "6pt"
                    }
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ]
